{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a11fabb5e95df704", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tDetection of AI-generated stems within hybrid human-AI music\n2\tTitle:\n3\tContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\n4\tDescription:\n5\tSubmit without GitHub\n6\tSubmit in GitHub\n7\tarXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\n8\tLearn more\n9\t×\n10\tLicense: CC BY 4.0\n11\tarXiv:2607.26874v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\n12\tDetection of AI-generated stems within\n13\thybrid human-AI music\n14\tAbstract\n15\tThis paper presents, to the best of our knowledge, the first study on detecting human-AI hybrid music tracks created by mixing human-produced and AI-generated stems.\n16\tBuilding on recent work showing that AI music detectors can identify decoder-related artifacts in fully generated music, we investigate whether such artifacts remain detectable at the stem level after mixing.\n17\tUsing MUSDB18-HQ database in a two-stem vocals + accompaniment setting, we simulate hybrid mixtures by autoencoding individual stems with a neural codec.\n18\tWe compare two strategies combining AI-generated mix detection and source separation. A naive sequential pipeline, where source separation is followed by detection on separated sources, confirms that artifacts associated with an AI-generated stem are not reliably recovered by generic source separation systems. We therefore propose a parallel architecture in which source separation is only used to estimate source-relative energy within the mixture. We then train simple stem-specific binary classifiers that take as input the generated mix prediction together with the relative energy of the target stem on short audio chunks. Averaging chunk-level predictions yields encouraging track-level results, highlighting the potential of such approaches for detecting AI-generated stems in hybrid music.\n19\t1\n20\tIntroduction\n21\tIn the past few years, music AI generation has become widespread, with numerous commercial services enabling users to create a full music song from a simple text prompt. Beyond concerns of copyright infringement related to model training and memorization\n22\t[\n23\t24\n24\t]\n25\t, the rapid adoption of these services resulted in the flooding of music streaming catalogs, associated for a significant part with fraudulent usage aimed at diverting revenues from actual artists\n26\t[\n27\t16\n28\t]\n29\t.\n30\tOne response to this challenge is automated detection,\n31\ti.e.\n32\tflagging whether a given track has been fully AI-generated.\n33\tRecent work has shown that AI-generated music can be detected with high accuracy, even in large-scale industrial contexts\n34\t[\n35\t2\n36\t,\n37\t1\n38\t]\n39\t. These systems exploit systematic artifacts introduced by the transposed convolution layers present in the neural decoders common to most generative models.\n40\tEarly AI music services were mostly limited to generate complete pieces from text prompts, yielding what we call\n41\tfully generated AI music\n42\t. Modern services, however, offer much finer control over the generated material. These platforms provide music producers with Digital Audio Workstation-like interfaces to interact with generative algorithms and produce individual layers of arrangement from an initial composition. Professional musicians and producers are increasingly incorporating such tools into their creative and production workflows, with major music labels partnering with generative AI services\n43\t[\n44\t23\n45\t,\n46\t21\n47\t,\n48\t22\n49\t]\n50\t.\n51\tIn this context,\n52\thybrid music\n53\t, for which some human-produced stems are mixed together with AI-generated stems, is expected to become increasingly common.\n54\tThis raises an important and unaddressed question:\n55\tcan one distinguish between fully generated AI music and hybrid music?\n56\tGoing beyond binary classification at the global level of a track, and detecting which stems are generated is of importance, as it may affect editorial policies, AI transparency, and artistic authenticity\n57\t[\n58\t13\n59\t]\n60\t.\n61\tTo the authors’ knowledge, no scientific methodology has been published on this task.\n62\tNote, that in this work, we restrict our definition of hybrid music to the audio recording level. Other configurations, such as human-written lyrics paired with a generated recording, or conversely, generated lyrics paired with a human recording\n63\t[\n64\t12\n65\t]\n66\t, fall outside the scope of this study.\n67\tIn this work, we focus on the limited two-stem setting of vocals and accompaniment mixtures, which still covers prominent hybrid scenarios enabled by modern generative platforms: AI-generated vocals imitating a specific voice layered over a real accompaniment, or conversely, an AI-generated accompaniment paired with a real vocal recording.\n68\tWe formalize the problem as a binary classification task for each stem independently, predicting whether it is AI-generated or not.\n69\tWhile results may vary for other stem types and mixing settings, in the following we provide insights into the factors likely to influence performance beyond these limitations and leave formal generalization to more stems for future work.\n70\tThis paper presents three main contributions.\n71\tFirst, we demonstrate experimentally in Section\n72\t4\n73\tthat state-of-the-art binary detectors fail to reliably handle hybrid mixes: depending on the source energy ratio, they either miss the generated stem or incorrectly flag the entire track.\n74\tSecond, in Section\n75\t5\n76\t, we evaluate a naive approach applying source separation prior to detection. We find that artifacts from generated stems are not reliably isolated by generic separation systems and spread over all separated stems, making this pipeline insufficient on its own.\n77\tThird, building on insights from these experiments, we propose in Section\n78\t6\n79\tlightweight stem-specific classifiers that take as input local mix-level detection scores and per-band source energy ratios. We show that aggregating chunk-level predictions yields substantially better stem-level performance than the naive approach.\n80\tTogether with this article, we provide a code repository\n81\t1\n82\t1\n83\t1\n84\tgithub.com/deezer/ismir26-hybrid-human-ai-music-detection\n85\tfor reproducing all the experiments.\n86\t2\n87\tRelated work\n88\tAI-Generated Music Detection:\n89\tAI-generated music detection has attracted increasing interest in recent years, with most approaches relying on black-box deep learning models that offer little understanding of their decision criteria\n90\t[\n91\t7\n92\t,\n93\t2\n94\t,\n95\t6\n96\t,\n97\t19\n98\t]\n99\t. Alternatively, checkerboard artifacts arising from transposed convolutions in the decoder have been identified as a reliable and interpretable detection signal\n100\t[\n101\t1\n102\t]\n103\t. These artifacts are a strong clue for discriminating generated music from genuine one. They are architecture-dependent and highly detectable, allowing a reliable binary classification that is consistently able to detect various models, including commercial ones\n104\t[\n105\t2\n106\t,\n107\t1\n108\t]\n109\t.\n110\tAn alternative paradigm frames detection as audio tagging,\n111\te.g.\n112\tusing CLAP embeddings\n113\t[\n114\t7\n115\t,\n116\t6\n117\t]\n118\t.\n119\tAll these approaches show significant vulnerability and drop in performance to transformations such as sample rate conversion, pitch shifting, or time stretching\n120\t[\n121\t6\n122\t,\n123\t2\n124\t,\n125\t1\n126\t]\n127\t.\n128\tDetection can also be achieved through lyrics, by exploiting statistical and semantic patterns of AI-generated text\n129\t[\n130\t12\n131\t,\n132\t11\n133\t]\n134\t, offering greater robustness to audio perturbations, but with limited performance.\n135\tBeyond detection, complementary work addresses transparency and data replication, using notably audio similarity metrics to assess whether AI-generated music reproduces training content\n136\t[\n137\t3\n138\t,\n139\t4\n140\t]\n141\t.\n142\tDatasets:\n143\tSeveral benchmarks have been proposed for AI music detection.\n144\tSONICS\n145\t[\n146\t19\n147\t]\n148\tcontains over 97k songs from Suno, Udio, and YouTube. While SONICS is a hybrid dataset at the prompt level with real or AI-generated lyrics/music styles, it does not mix human-recorded and AI-generated stems at the audio level.\n149\tFakeMusicCaps\n150\t[\n151\t5\n152\t]\n153\ttargets both detection and attribution using five open-source text-to-music models.\n154\tArtifactBench\n155\t[\n156\t17\n157\t]\n158\tspans 22 generators for codec-aware generalization evaluation, and M6\n159\t[\n160\t14\n161\t]\n162\tcovers multiple generators, domains, languages, genres, and instruments.\n163\tAI-OpenBMAT\n164\t[\n165\t15\n166\t]\n167\taddresses broadcast scenarios with short, speech-masked excerpts, reporting F1-scores below 60% when the SNR of the fake signal is low. This setting is related to ours, as AI-generated stems are mixed with human-recorded sources, but, as opposed to our study, the human-recorded part is considered as noise preventing detection.\n168\tNone of those datasets include\n169\thybrid\n170\tmusic that mixes AI-generated stems with real stems.\n171\tGenerated Singing Voice Detection in Music Mixtures:\n172\tThe task of detecting whether vocals in a music track are real or AI-generated is the most closely related to ours. SingFake\n173\t[\n174\t25\n175\t]\n176\taddresses this by training several anti-spoofing countermeasure systems, either on source-separated vocals or full mixtures, finding that the benefit of source separation prior to detection is architecture-dependent, with no consistent conclusion across systems. Similarly, cloned singer identification shows that mixture-based models degrade substantially more than vocal-based ones when exposed to cloned voices\n177\t[\n178\t10\n179\t]\n180\t. A key distinction from both works is that our task is more general: rather than targeting just vocal authenticity, we perform\n181\tstem-level\n182\tdetection for potentially any component of the mix.\n183\t3\n184\tHybrid music dataset\n185\tTo our knowledge, no dataset currently exists for hybrid music containing musically coherent stems from real and AI-generated sources.\n186\tWhile building a realistic database in such settings from commercial AI generation services would require a dedicated work (left for future iterations), we decide here to synthesize hybrid mixture through a neural autoencoding of stems taken from a music source separation dataset, following the method in\n187\t[\n188\t1\n189\t,\n190\t2\n191\t]\n192\t.\n193\tWe use MUSDB18-HQ\n194\t[\n195\t18\n196\t]\n197\tsource separation dataset providing multi-stems for 150 tracks, downmixed to isolated vocals\n198\tv\n199\tv\n200\tand accompaniment\n201\ta\n202\ta\n203\t.\n204\tIn order to synthesize the AI generation of a stem, we apply\n205\tencodec\n206\tautoencoding with 24 kbps at 48 kHz configuration\n207\t[\n208\t9\n209\t]\n210\t.\n211\tThis follows the framework introduced in\n212\t[\n213\t1\n214\t]\n215\t, on which we base our work, and that reported robust detection performance for 3 reference neural codecs, as well as 3 versions of Suno and one of Udio.\n216\tWe thus hypothesize that our experimental work and findings based on a single decoder should provide a robust base framework for extending the experiments to more realistic hybrid mixtures.\n217\tFrom both the original (with subscript\n218\tr\n219\tr\n220\tfor\n221\treal\n222\t) and the autoencoded (\n223\tg\n224\tg\n225\tfor\n226\tgenerated\n227\t) stems of a given track, we construct four types of coherent mixes:\n228\t•\n229\tHybrid mix with generated vocals\n230\t: encoded vocals with non-encoded accompaniment, noted\n231\tv\n232\tg\n233\t+\n234\ta\n235\tr\n236\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n237\t.\n238\t•\n239\tHybrid mix with generated accompaniment\n240\t: non-encoded vocals with encoded accompaniment,\n241\tv\n242\tr\n243\t+\n244\ta\n245\tg\n246\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n247\t.\n248\t•\n249\tFully generated mix\n250\t: both stems encoded,\n251\tv\n252\tg\n253\t+\n254\ta\n255\tg\n256\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n257\t.\n258\t•\n259\tGenuine mix\n260\t: both stems non-encoded,\n261\tv\n262\tr\n263\t+\n264\ta\n265\tr\n266\tv_{r}+a_{r}\n267\t.\n268\tThe mixes are constructed linearly with different mixing gains.\n269\tIn the remainder of this paper, when evaluating stem-specific detectors, we define as\n270\ttarget gain\n271\tin\n272\t{\n273\t−\n274\t12\n275\t,\n276\t−\n277\t6\n278\t,\n279\t0\n280\t,\n281\t+\n282\t6\n283\t,\n284\t+\n285\t12\n286\t}\n287\t\\{-12,-6,0,+6,+12\\}\n288\tdB, the gain applied to the target stem (\n289\te.g\n290\tthe vocals for an AI vocals detection task) while the other stem’s gain is set to 0 dB (\n291\te.g\n292\tthe accompaniment).\n293\tFinally, a peak normalization to unit amplitude is applied to the obtained mixture.\n294\tMore complex mixing scenarios, including mastering, are left for future work.\n295\tThe full dataset comprises\n296\t150\n297\t150\n298\tmixtures\n299\t×\n300\t4\n301\t\\times 4\n302\tmix types\n303\t×\n304\t5\n305\t\\times 5\n306\ttarget gains\n307\t=\n308\t3\n309\t,\n310\t000\n311\t=3{,}000\n312\tmixtures.\n313\ttarget gain (dB)\n314\t-12\n315\t-6\n316\t0\n317\t6\n318\t12\n319\tv\n320\tg\n321\t+\n322\ta\n323\tr\n324\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n325\t12.0\n326\t31.33\n327\t55.33\n328\t78.67\n329\t88.67\n330\tv\n331\tr\n332\t+\n333\ta\n334\tg\n335\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n336\t97.33\n337\t98.67\n338\t98.67\n339\t99.33\n340\t100.0\n341\tTable 1\n342\t:\n343\tProportion (in %) of tracks detected as fully generated when the reference detector is directly applied to hybrid mixtures, for different mixing gains.\n344\t4\n345\tFully generated AI music detection\n346\tThis section details the training and evaluation of fully generated AI music detectors that will be used in Sections\n347\t5\n348\tand\n349\t6\n350\tfor building stem-specific detectors.\n351\t4.1\n352\tReference detector\n353\tIn this preliminary experiment, we assess the limits of an artifact-based AI detector trained from real and fully generated music, when applied to hybrid music.\n354\tThus, here the task is a global \"generated\n355\tvs.\n356\treal\" binary classification of a track, with no detection at the stem level.\n357\tTraining on fully generated music:\n358\tAs our reference AI detector, we reproduce the system from\n359\t[\n360\t1\n361\t]\n362\t.\n363\tWe train this detector from scratch using the raw audio files from the Free Music Archive (FMA) medium dataset\n364\t[\n365\t8\n366\t]\n367\tas the negative class (real), and the same files autoencoded with\n368\tencodec\n369\t[\n370\t9\n371\t]\n372\tas the positive class (generated).\n373\tWe use a random train/test split of 70/30.\n374\tBoth real and generated versions of a track are distributed in the same set.\n375\tOn the test set, we obtain a True Positive Rate (TPR) of\n376\t99.79\n377\t%\n378\t99.79\\%\n379\tand a False Positive Rate (FPR) of\n380\t0\n381\t%\n382\t0\\%\n383\t, which is consistent with\n384\t[\n385\t1\n386\t]\n387\t. Note that this task is performed on mixes only: the detector is a single binary classifier that discriminates fully real\n388\tvs.\n389\tfully generated tracks, and does not provide any information about the generation at the stem level.\n390\tInterestingly, when testing on the (unseen) dataset from Section\n391\t3\n392\ton\n393\tv\n394\tr\n395\t+\n396\ta\n397\tr\n398\tv_{r}+a_{r}\n399\tand\n400\tv\n401\tg\n402\t+\n403\ta\n404\tg\n405\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n406\tscenarios, the detector achieves perfect accuracy (\n407\t100\n408\t%\n409\t100\\%\n410\tTPR and\n411\t0\n412\t%\n413\t0\\%\n414\tFPR).\n415\tTesting on hybrid mixtures:\n416\tHere we apply the reference AI music detector to hybrid tracks,\n417\ti.e.\n418\tto\n419\tv\n420\tr\n421\t+\n422\ta\n423\tg\n424\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n425\tand\n426\tv\n427\tg\n428\t+\n429\ta\n430\tr\n431\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n432\tsubsets from the dataset of Section\n433\t3\n434\t.\n435\tThe results, presented in\n436\tTable\n437\t1\n438\t, show that the artifacts of the generated stem persist in the hybrid mixtures and that their detection is influenced by the target gain and the nature of the stem source. We observe an asymmetry between vocals and accompaniment with more detection in the case\n439\tv\n440\tr\n441\t+\n442\ta\n443\tg\n444\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n445\t. This is probably because accompaniments that include percussion are usually dominant in high frequency bands where the detection happens (5-16kHz).\n446\tVocals also tend to be sparser over time and might be a minor contributor to the average profile computed by the detection system\n447\t[\n448\t1\n449\t]\n450\t.\n451\tTherefore, while a plain binary detector is unable to distinguish between hybrid and fully generated mixes, this preliminary result suggests that useful detection artifacts are preserved during the mixing operation.\n452\t4.2\n453\tFrom global to local AI detection\n454\tWe here adapt the fully generated AI detector, as presented above, from a global (full track prediction) to a local scale (short segment prediction).\n455\tThis will allow for a finer temporal granularity essential to our proposed approach, as described in the following sections.\n456\tWe thus re-train detectors on short audio chunks of\n457\t1\n458\t1\n459\t,\n460\t2\n461\t2\n462\t,\n463\t5\n464\t5\n465\tand\n466\t10\n467\t10\n468\tseconds from the same FMA-based dataset, keeping the same experimental conditions.\n469\tTable\n470\t2\n471\treports the AI chunk detection performance for all configurations.\n472\tPerformance remains high with only limited degradations for very short segments where only a fraction of audio is provided.\n473\tChunk dur. (s)\n474\t10\n475\t5\n476\t2\n477\t1\n478\tTPR (%)\n479\t99.733\n480\t99.693\n481\t99.139\n482\t96.860\n483\tFPR (%)\n484\t0.025\n485\t0.081\n486\t0.516\n487\t2.015\n488\tTable 2\n489\t:\n490\tFully AI-generated chunk prediction performance as a function of the audio chunk duration (in seconds).\n491\t5\n492\tDetection of AI-generated stems: A naive approach\n493\tWe now turn to the main task addressed in this work, namely AI-generated stem detection, and introduce a method that provides a prediction score for each stem (vocals or accompaniment) indicating whether or not it was generated.\n494\tThis section explores a naive baseline solution to this task that consists of performing Music Source Separation (MSS) and then applying a plain AI music detector independently to each stem.\n495\t5.1\n496\tBaseline: separate then detect\n497\tHere, we use the dataset presented in Section\n498\t3\n499\t, considering the four mixing scenarios.\n500\tWe first apply\n501\tht-demucs\n502\t[\n503\t20\n504\t]\n505\tto perform 2-stems MSS on the mixtures, and then apply the global reference detector from Section\n506\t4.1\n507\tindependently on each isolated stem.\n508\tThe TPR and FPR for both generated vocals and accompaniment detection tasks are reported as blue bars in\n509\tFigure\n510\t2\n511\tfor the different target gains.\n512\tOverall, the performance is quite poor, especially for the detection of generated vocals.\n513\tOnce again, the performance varies monotonically with respect to the mixing gain of the target stem, with very high FPRs for both vocals and accompaniment detection at lower target gains.\n514\tConfusion matrices, represented in\n515\tFigure\n516\t1\n517\t, highlight that for the hybrid case\n518\tv\n519\tr\n520\t+\n521\ta\n522\tg\n523\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n524\t, the real vocals are almost always predicted as generated with a FPR of\n525\t94.7\n526\t%\n527\t94.7\\%\n528\t(left matrix).\n529\tThis suggests that artifacts from the generated accompaniment may have spread in the vocal stem through MSS.\n530\tThus, this naive system is unable to discriminate between\n531\tv\n532\tr\n533\t+\n534\ta\n535\tg\n536\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n537\tand\n538\tv\n539\tg\n540\t+\n541\ta\n542\tg\n543\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n544\t.\n545\tThe discrimination between\n546\tv\n547\tg\n548\t+\n549\ta\n550\tr\n551\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n552\tand\n553\tv\n554\tg\n555\t+\n556\ta\n557\tg\n558\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n559\tis also weak, with a FPR of\n560\t38\n561\t%\n562\t38\\%\n563\tfor the detection of accompaniment in the hybrid case\n564\tv\n565\tg\n566\t+\n567\ta\n568\tr\n569\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n570\t.\n571\tFigure 1\n572\t:\n573\tConfusion matrices for the naive stem detectors for all mixing cases with target gain\n574\t0\n575\tdB. Correct (resp. False) detection cases are colored in green (resp. red).\n576\t5.2\n577\tQualitative analysis from the local AI detection\n578\tWe hypothesize that the naive approach performs poorly due to the MSS inability to accurately transfer the artifacts to the generated stem only, and propose a qualitative analysis based on the local AI-generated music detector from Section\n579\t4.2\n580\t.\n581\tFigures\n582\t5\n583\t(a) and (b) illustrate the case of a hybrid track with generated vocals (\n584\tv\n585\tg\n586\t+\n587\ta\n588\tr\n589\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n590\t).\n591\tInterestingly, local detection scores from the mix (blue), vocals (green) and accompaniment (orange) from subfigure (b) are highly correlated, while we would expect here that the accompaniment score remains close to 0 along the whole excerpt.\n592\tThis tends to confirm our assumption that generation artifacts are spread over all separated sources.\n593\tWe must note here that the MSS system can also introduce its own artifacts, potentially of a similar nature to those expected from neural codecs.\n594\tIndeed,\n595\tht-demucs\n596\t[\n597\t20\n598\t]\n599\tis based on an encoder/decoder-like architecture, involving upsampling from a latent space with transposed convolution layers.\n600\tWhile such artifacts are likely to appear, it seems from Figure\n601\t5\n602\t(b) that this did not perturb our experiments: both local detection scores from extracted vocal and accompaniment stems return to 0 after\n603\t177\n604\t~177\n605\ts\n606\ts\n607\t,\n608\ti.e.\n609\twhen the generated stem is inactive.\n610\tWhile we could consider several, probably limited, improvements to this naive baseline (\n611\te.g.\n612\ttesting different MSS architectures, retraining a MSS model from hybrid mixtures, or more simply retraining stem-specific detectors from separated sources), in the following section we propose an alternative approach that helps circumventing the exposed weaknesses of this sequential approach.\n613\t6\n614\tProposed approach\n615\tBuilding on preliminary experiments described in Section\n616\t5\n617\t, we propose here a joint/parallel approach that combines the prediction of the local mix detector with the information about the relative time-frequency energy of each stem.\n618\t6.1\n619\tFormalizing the task\n620\tIndependently for each stem, we design a binary classifier that predicts from the audio mixture, at the chunk level (indexed by\n621\tt\n622\tt\n623\t), whether the considered stem was generated or not.\n624\tThe goal is to compute an estimate of\n625\tp\n626\t​\n627\t(\n628\tv\n629\tg\n630\t|\n631\tf\n632\tt\n633\t,\n634\t{\n635\tSNR\n636\tt\n637\tb\n638\t,\n639\tb\n640\t∈\n641\tB\n642\t}\n643\t)\n644\tp(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n645\t(resp.\n646\tp\n647\t​\n648\t(\n649\ta\n650\tg\n651\t|\n652\tf\n653\tt\n654\t,\n655\t{\n656\tSNR\n657\tt\n658\tb\n659\t,\n660\tb\n661\t∈\n662\tB\n663\t}\n664\t)\n665\tp(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n666\t), the posterior probability that the vocal (resp. the accompaniment) stem is generated, given the prediction of the local AI mix detector\n667\tf\n668\tt\n669\tf_{t}\n670\t, and the ratio of energy between the target and complementary stems\n671\tSNR\n672\tt\n673\tb\n674\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n675\tfor each band\n676\tb\n677\tb\n678\tin the set of all frequency bands considered\n679\tB\n680\tB\n681\t.\n682\tf\n683\tt\n684\tf_{t}\n685\tis obtained directly as the output of the local detector of Section\n686\t4.2\n687\tapplied to the input mix chunk\n688\tt\n689\tt\n690\t.\n691\tThe energy quantities used to compute\n692\tSNR\n693\tt\n694\tb\n695\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n696\tare defined as the sum of the squared magnitudes of the short-time Fourier transform over the considered stems at chunk\n697\tt\n698\tt\n699\tand frequency band\n700\tb\n701\tb\n702\t.\n703\tWe consider two scenarios for estimating\n704\tSNR\n705\tt\n706\tb\n707\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n708\t:\n709\t•\n710\tMSS SNR\n711\t, where we apply\n712\tht-demucs\n713\ton the mix and use the separated stems to compute the source energies.\n714\t•\n715\tOracle SNR\n716\t, where the energy of the sources is directly computed from the available reference stems of the dataset, therefore bypassing the potential degradations and artifacts introduced by MSS.\n717\tIn the following, we train a model at the chunk level\n718\tt\n719\tt\n720\t(thus considering the independence of chunks) for\n721\tp\n722\t​\n723\t(\n724\tv\n725\tg\n726\t|\n727\tf\n728\tt\n729\t,\n730\t{\n731\tSNR\n732\tt\n733\tb\n734\t,\n735\tb\n736\t∈\n737\tB\n738\t}\n739\t)\n740\tp(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n741\tand\n742\tp\n743\t​\n744\t(\n745\ta\n746\tg\n747\t|\n748\tf\n749\tt\n750\t,\n751\t{\n752\tSNR\n753\tt\n754\tb\n755\t,\n756\tb\n757\t∈\n758\tB\n759\t}\n760\t)\n761\tp(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n762\t.\n763\tThen, in order to get a prediction at the level of the full track, we compute the average over time of these probabilities and detect the stem as generated if it is above\n764\t0.5\n765\t0.5\n766\t, and as not generated otherwise.\n767\t6.2\n768\tExperiments\n769\tWe use\n770\t2\n771\t2\n772\t-second-long chunks with\n773\t90\n774\t%\n775\t90\\%\n776\toverlap between chunks.\n777\tWe drop chunks with very low energy, considered as silence, as the corresponding\n778\tf\n779\tt\n780\tf_{t}\n781\tand\n782\tSNR\n783\tt\n784\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}\n785\tare unreliable in both train and test sets.\n786\tThis pre-processing removes on average\n787\t∼\n788\t5\n789\t%\n790\t\\sim 5\\%\n791\tof chunks per track, essentially trimming leading and trailing silences on the mix.\n792\tWe train a simple multilayer perceptron model (5 hidden layers, 32 linear units each, ReLu activation, sigmoid on output layer) for AI-generated vocals detection and a second independent one for AI-generated accompaniment detection.\n793\tWe use binary cross-entropy as loss function.\n794\tf\n795\tt\n796\tf_{t}\n797\tand\n798\tSNR\n799\tt\n800\tb\n801\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n802\tinput features are simply stacked together (total dimension of\n803\tB\n804\t+\n805\t1\n806\tB+1\n807\t) as input to the model.\n808\tWe run 10 cross-validation 70/30 splits, where the split is applied at the track level (\n809\ti.e.\n810\tall mixing variants of a given track are distributed into the same set).\n811\tFor the SNR bands, we use two configurations:\n812\t•\n813\t16 bands with 1 kHz width over the range 0-16 kHz, used for reporting performance in\n814\t6.3\n815\t.\n816\t•\n817\tA single band 5-16 kHz (which corresponds to the band used in\n818\t[\n819\t1\n820\t]\n821\tfor computing the detector’s input features), only used for the qualitative analysis in Section\n822\t6.4\n823\t.\n824\tFigure 2\n825\t:\n826\tTPR (top) and FPR (bottom) metrics for vocals (left) and accompaniment (right) AI-generated stem detections grouped over all mixing configurations, and as a function of the target gain (x-axis).\n827\t6.3\n828\tEvaluation\n829\tTPRs and FPRs are reported in\n830\tFigure\n831\t2\n832\t.\n833\tWe can see that the performance in the Oracle scenario (green bars) is quite high.\n834\tAs expected again, we observe that both metrics degrade as the target gain decreases: detection becomes more difficult when the generated stem has lower energy.\n835\tWe also observe that performance is stem-dependent and that it remains much easier to detect generated accompaniment than generated vocals.\n836\tIn the MSS scenario, general trends are similar (orange bars).\n837\tTPRs are comparable to the Oracle configuration, but we notice a significant FPR degradation for the prediction of generated vocals, especially at low vocals gain.\n838\tIn this realistic configuration, however, performance remains substantially better than the naive method (blue bars).\n839\tFor a more detailed analysis of errors from the the vocals detector, we display in\n840\tFigure\n841\t3\n842\tthe confusion matrices against the\n843\t4\n844\t4\n845\tpossible mixing classes at target gain 0dB, for both SNR scenarios.\n846\tWe notice that, once again, the most difficult case is the discrimination between the hybrid mix\n847\tv\n848\tr\n849\t+\n850\ta\n851\tg\n852\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n853\tand the fully generated mix\n854\tv\n855\tg\n856\t+\n857\ta\n858\tg\n859\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n860\t.\n861\tThe realistic system using SNR estimated from MSS achieves the most degraded performance when compared to the Oracle SNR configuration.\n862\tHowever, as opposed to the naive method (Fig.\n863\t1\n864\t, left matrix), our system shows a stronger ability to discriminate between the hybrid case\n865\tv\n866\tr\n867\t+\n868\ta\n869\tg\n870\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n871\tand the fully generated case\n872\tv\n873\tg\n874\t+\n875\ta\n876\tg\n877\tv_{g}+a_{g}\n878\t.\n879\tEven in the realistic scenario using MSS-based SNR, for hybrid mixes\n880\tv\n881\tr\n882\t+\n883\ta\n884\tg\n885\tv_{r}+a_{g}\n886\tthe FPR drops from\n887\t94.7\n888\t%\n889\t94.7\\%\n890\tfor the naive approach to\n891\t26.2\n892\t%\n893\t26.2\\%\n894\tfor the proposed approach.\n895\tFigure 3\n896\t:\n897\tConfusion matrices for the proposed AI-generated vocals detector for a target gain of\n898\t0\n899\tdB. Influence of MSS (right) over Oracle (left) SNR estimator.\n900\tWe must acknowledge here that Demucs was trained on MUSDB18-HQ.\n901\tThis could possibly result in better local SNR estimates than on data not seen during Demucs training, and in turn lead to overoptimistic performance.\n902\tThis would require retraining Demucs with other data, or collecting another dataset, which is left for future work.\n903\t6.4\n904\tQualitative analysis\n905\tIn\n906\tFigure\n907\t4\n908\t, we display the trained model posteriors\n909\tp\n910\t​\n911\t(\n912\tv\n913\tg\n914\t|\n915\tf\n916\tt\n917\t,\n918\tSNR\n919\tt\n920\tb\n921\t)\n922\tp(v_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\n923\tand\n924\tp\n925\t​\n926\t(\n927\ta\n928\tg\n929\t|\n930\tf\n931\tt\n932\t,\n933\tSNR\n934\tt\n935\tb\n936\t)\n937\tp(a_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\n938\tfor the simple case where there is a single SNR band from 5 kHz to 16 kHz.\n939\tThe x-axis shows the logit-transformed local mix detection score\n940\tlogit\n941\t​\n942\t(\n943\tf\n944\tt\n945\t)\n946\t=\n947\tlog\n948\t⁡\n949\t(\n950\tf\n951\tt\n952\t/\n953\t(\n954\t1\n955\t−\n956\tf\n957\tt\n958\t)\n959\t)\n960\t\\text{logit}(f_{t})=\\log(f_{t}/(1-f_{t}))\n961\t, mapping the output from\n962\t[\n963\t0\n964\t,\n965\t1\n966\t]\n967\t[0,1]\n968\tto\n969\t]\n970\t−\n971\t∞\n972\t,\n973\t+\n974\t∞\n975\t[\n976\t]-\\infty,+\\infty[\n977\t, where negative (resp. positive) values indicate that the local mix detector predicts the segment as real (resp. generated).\n978\tThe y-axis represents the target stem SNR in dB.\n979\tAlthough the decision frontiers are stem-dependent, a few consistent areas can be interpreted:\n980\tArea 1:\n981\tfor\n982\tSNR\n983\t≫\n984\t0\n985\t\\textrm{SNR}\\gg 0\n986\t, the target stem (noted\n987\ts\n988\t∈\n989\t{\n990\tv\n991\t,\n992\ta\n993\t}\n994\ts\\in\\{v,a\\}\n995\t) is the dominant stem, then the prediction of generation for this stem tends towards the prediction of the mix:\n996\tp\n997\t​\n998\t(\n999\ts\n1000\tg\n1001\t|\n1002\tf\n1003\tt\n1004\t,\n1005\tSNR\n1006\tt\n1007\tb\n1008\t)\n1009\t→\n1010\tSNR\n1011\tt\n1012\tb\n1013\t→\n1014\t+\n1015\t∞\n1016\tf\n1017\tt\n1018\tp(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\xrightarrow[\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\\rightarrow+\\infty]{}f_{t}\n1019\t.\n1020\tFor\n1021\tSNR\n1022\t≪\n1023\t0\n1024\t\\textrm{SNR}\\ll 0\n1025\t,\n1026\ti.e.\n1027\twhen the complementary stem is dominant, we observe three main areas:\n1028\tArea 2:\n1029\tfor middle prediction scores on the mix (\n1030\t0\n1031\t≪\n1032\tf\n1033\tt\n1034\t≪\n1035\t1\n1036\t0\\ll f_{t}\\ll 1\n1037\t),\n1038\ti.e.\n1039\twhen the mix detector is less confident, the target stem which has lower energy is likely generated.\n1040\tOur interpretation is the following: for middle prediction scores in this specific 2-stem mixture scenario, at least one and only one stem has to be generated.\n1041\tIndeed, if both were real (resp. generated) the local mix detection score would tend towards 0 (resp. 1).\n1042\tThe complementary stem is here dominant and thus less likely to be generated; if it was, it would trigger a greater prediction score towards 1.\n1043\tArea 3:\n1044\tf\n1045\tt\n1046\t→\n1047\t1\n1048\tf_{t}\\rightarrow 1\n1049\t, the detector’s score on the mix is very high.\n1050\tIt is then likely that either the mix is fully generated, or the complementary dominant stem only is generated.\n1051\tThe target stem then has equal chances to be generated or real, hence\n1052\tp\n1053\t​\n1054\t(\n1055\ts\n1056\tg\n1057\t|\n1058\tf\n1059\tt\n1060\t,\n1061\tSNR\n1062\tt\n1063\tb\n1064\t)\n1065\t∼\n1066\t0.5\n1067\tp(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\sim 0.5\n1068\t.\n1069\tNote that this value is due to the balance of the 4 classes of mix in our dataset, and can be interpreted as an uninformative prior.\n1070\tArea 4:\n1071\tf\n1072\tt\n1073\t→\n1074\t0\n1075\tf_{t}\\rightarrow 0\n1076\t, the detector on the mix has a very low prediction value. This means either that both stems are real, or that the energy of the target stem is so low that its generation artifacts disappear in the mix. Then, the target stem\n1077\ts\n1078\ts\n1079\tcan either be generated or real, and\n1080\tp\n1081\t​\n1082\t(\n1083\ts\n1084\tg\n1085\t|\n1086\tf\n1087\tt\n1088\t,\n1089\tSNR\n1090\tt\n1091\tb\n1092\t)\n1093\t∼\n1094\t0.5\n1095\tp(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\sim 0.5\n1096\t.\n1097\tThis phenomenon appears mostly for vocals, though. For accompaniment,\n1098\tp\n1099\t​\n1100\t(\n1101\ta\n1102\tg\n1103\t|\n1104\tf\n1105\tt\n1106\t,\n1107\tSNR\n1108\tt\n1109\tb\n1110\t)\n1111\tp(a_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\n1112\tdrops to\n1113\t0\n1114\t, which is likely due to the fact that mixing with a vocals stem, even with high energy, is insufficient to hide generation artifacts (possibly from drums sitting in the hi-frequency range).\n1115\tAgain, the value\n1116\t0.5\n1117\t0.5\n1118\tcan still be interpreted as an uninformative prior.\n1119\tThis analysis gives an overview of the informative zones for differentiating hybrid mixes. Some of them are intuitive: in an area where a stem is strongly dominant, the prediction on the mix transfers to the stem.\n1120\tHowever, some are counterintuitive, as one may consider a low energy ratio for the target stem to result in an uninformative zone, when such a ratio can actually be very informative for middle-valued detection scores.\n1121\tAdditional tests suggested that these properties remain valid in the case of\n1122\tSNR\n1123\tt\n1124\tb\n1125\t\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n1126\testimated for multiple bands\n1127\tb\n1128\tb\n1129\t.\n1130\tThese conclusions also describe hard samples: these are tracks for which the target stem SNR remains low while the prediction score\n1131\tf\n1132\tt\n1133\tf_{t}\n1134\tremains extreme.\n1135\tOn such samples, the posterior of the low-energy stem is uninformative and does not enable the discrimination between a fully generated mix and a hybrid mix where only one stem with high energy was generated.\n1136\tThe larger zone for hard samples in\n1137\tp\n1138\t​\n1139\t(\n1140\tv\n1141\tg\n1142\t|\n1143\tf\n1144\tt\n1145\t,\n1146\tSNR\n1147\tt\n1148\tb\n1149\t)\n1150\tp(v_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\n1151\tmay explain the loss of performance between vocals and accompaniment stem detection.\n1152\tFigure 4\n1153\t:\n1154\tTrained posteriors for the generated vocals (left) and accompaniment (right) detection models.\n1155\tX-axis: logit-transformed fake score; how strongly the local detector predicts the mix as fake.\n1156\tY-axis: SNR in dB; how loud the target stem is relative to the complementary stem.\n1157\tFigure\n1158\t5\n1159\t(c) depicts the local estimation of\n1160\tp\n1161\t​\n1162\t(\n1163\tv\n1164\tg\n1165\t|\n1166\tf\n1167\tt\n1168\t,\n1169\t{\n1170\tSNR\n1171\tt\n1172\tb\n1173\t,\n1174\tb\n1175\t∈\n1176\tB\n1177\t}\n1178\t)\n1179\tp(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n1180\tand\n1181\tp\n1182\t​\n1183\t(\n1184\ta\n1185\tg\n1186\t|\n1187\tf\n1188\tt\n1189\t,\n1190\t{\n1191\tSNR\n1192\tt\n1193\tb\n1194\t,\n1195\tb\n1196\t∈\n1197\tB\n1198\t}\n1199\t)\n1200\tp(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n1201\ton a hybrid track\n1202\tv\n1203\tg\n1204\t+\n1205\ta\n1206\tr\n1207\tv_{g}+a_{r}\n1208\t(generated vocals and real accompaniment).\n1209\tThe estimation here is consistently correct with a high generation probability for vocals and a low generation probability for accompaniment.\n1210\tThe system becomes less confident for the accompaniment when the vocal activity decreases.\n1211\tHowever, after\n1212\t177\n1213\t​\n1214\ts\n1215\t177s\n1216\t, when only the accompaniment is active, the probability for fake vocals approaches\n1217\t0.5\n1218\t0.5\n1219\t(uninformative chunks, in accordance with leftmost\n1220\tFigure\n1221\t4\n1222\t, Area 4), and the probability for fake accompaniment drops close to\n1223\t0\n1224\t(low score \n1225\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "accca8a2-ed0e-449e-a1b3-ef044a4b62b6", "uuid": "0f4428cc-8ac4-4357-8b79-e8f43f2b6157", "timestamp": "2026-07-30T05:47:07.467Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/accca8a2-ed0e-449e-a1b3-ef044a4b62b6/tool-results/bzindq6he.txt", "content": "Detection of AI-generated stems within hybrid human-AI music\nTitle:\nContent selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nDescription:\nSubmit without GitHub\nSubmit in GitHub\narXiv is now an independent nonprofit!\nLearn more\n×\nLicense: CC BY 4.0\narXiv:2607.26874v1 [cs.SD] 29 Jul 2026\nDetection of AI-generated stems within\nhybrid human-AI music\nAbstract\nThis paper presents, to the best of our knowledge, the first study on detecting human-AI hybrid music tracks created by mixing human-produced and AI-generated stems.\nBuilding on recent work showing that AI music detectors can identify decoder-related artifacts in fully generated music, we investigate whether such artifacts remain detectable at the stem level after mixing.\nUsing MUSDB18-HQ database in a two-stem vocals + accompaniment setting, we simulate hybrid mixtures by autoencoding individual stems with a neural codec.\nWe compare two strategies combining AI-generated mix detection and source separation. A naive sequential pipeline, where source separation is followed by detection on separated sources, confirms that artifacts associated with an AI-generated stem are not reliably recovered by generic source separation systems. We therefore propose a parallel architecture in which source separation is only used to estimate source-relative energy within the mixture. We then train simple stem-specific binary classifiers that take as input the generated mix prediction together with the relative energy of the target stem on short audio chunks. Averaging chunk-level predictions yields encouraging track-level results, highlighting the potential of such approaches for detecting AI-generated stems in hybrid music.\n1\nIntroduction\nIn the past few years, music AI generation has become widespread, with numerous commercial services enabling users to create a full music song from a simple text prompt. Beyond concerns of copyright infringement related to model training and memorization\n[\n24\n]\n, the rapid adoption of these services resulted in the flooding of music streaming catalogs, associated for a significant part with fraudulent usage aimed at diverting revenues from actual artists\n[\n16\n]\n.\nOne response to this challenge is automated detection,\ni.e.\nflagging whether a given track has been fully AI-generated.\nRecent work has shown that AI-generated music can be detected with high accuracy, even in large-scale industrial contexts\n[\n2\n,\n1\n]\n. These systems exploit systematic artifacts introduced by the transposed convolution layers present in the neural decoders common to most generative models.\nEarly AI music services were mostly limited to generate complete pieces from text prompts, yielding what we call\nfully generated AI music\n. Modern services, however, offer much finer control over the generated material. These platforms provide music producers with Digital Audio Workstation-like interfaces to interact with generative algorithms and produce individual layers of arrangement from an initial composition. Professional musicians and producers are increasingly incorporating such tools into their creative and production workflows, with major music labels partnering with generative AI services\n[\n23\n,\n21\n,\n22\n]\n.\nIn this context,\nhybrid music\n, for which some human-produced stems are mixed together with AI-generated stems, is expected to become increasingly common.\nThis raises an important and unaddressed question:\ncan one distinguish between fully generated AI music and hybrid music?\nGoing beyond binary classification at the global level of a track, and detecting which stems are generated is of importance, as it may affect editorial policies, AI transparency, and artistic authenticity\n[\n13\n]\n.\nTo the authors’ knowledge, no scientific methodology has been published on this task.\nNote, that in this work, we restrict our definition of hybrid music to the audio recording level. Other configurations, such as human-written lyrics paired with a generated recording, or conversely, generated lyrics paired with a human recording\n[\n12\n]\n, fall outside the scope of this study.\nIn this work, we focus on the limited two-stem setting of vocals and accompaniment mixtures, which still covers prominent hybrid scenarios enabled by modern generative platforms: AI-generated vocals imitating a specific voice layered over a real accompaniment, or conversely, an AI-generated accompaniment paired with a real vocal recording.\nWe formalize the problem as a binary classification task for each stem independently, predicting whether it is AI-generated or not.\nWhile results may vary for other stem types and mixing settings, in the following we provide insights into the factors likely to influence performance beyond these limitations and leave formal generalization to more stems for future work.\nThis paper presents three main contributions.\nFirst, we demonstrate experimentally in Section\n4\nthat state-of-the-art binary detectors fail to reliably handle hybrid mixes: depending on the source energy ratio, they either miss the generated stem or incorrectly flag the entire track.\nSecond, in Section\n5\n, we evaluate a naive approach applying source separation prior to detection. We find that artifacts from generated stems are not reliably isolated by generic separation systems and spread over all separated stems, making this pipeline insufficient on its own.\nThird, building on insights from these experiments, we propose in Section\n6\nlightweight stem-specific classifiers that take as input local mix-level detection scores and per-band source energy ratios. We show that aggregating chunk-level predictions yields substantially better stem-level performance than the naive approach.\nTogether with this article, we provide a code repository\n1\n1\n1\ngithub.com/deezer/ismir26-hybrid-human-ai-music-detection\nfor reproducing all the experiments.\n2\nRelated work\nAI-Generated Music Detection:\nAI-generated music detection has attracted increasing interest in recent years, with most approaches relying on black-box deep learning models that offer little understanding of their decision criteria\n[\n7\n,\n2\n,\n6\n,\n19\n]\n. Alternatively, checkerboard artifacts arising from transposed convolutions in the decoder have been identified as a reliable and interpretable detection signal\n[\n1\n]\n. These artifacts are a strong clue for discriminating generated music from genuine one. They are architecture-dependent and highly detectable, allowing a reliable binary classification that is consistently able to detect various models, including commercial ones\n[\n2\n,\n1\n]\n.\nAn alternative paradigm frames detection as audio tagging,\ne.g.\nusing CLAP embeddings\n[\n7\n,\n6\n]\n.\nAll these approaches show significant vulnerability and drop in performance to transformations such as sample rate conversion, pitch shifting, or time stretching\n[\n6\n,\n2\n,\n1\n]\n.\nDetection can also be achieved through lyrics, by exploiting statistical and semantic patterns of AI-generated text\n[\n12\n,\n11\n]\n, offering greater robustness to audio perturbations, but with limited performance.\nBeyond detection, complementary work addresses transparency and data replication, using notably audio similarity metrics to assess whether AI-generated music reproduces training content\n[\n3\n,\n4\n]\n.\nDatasets:\nSeveral benchmarks have been proposed for AI music detection.\nSONICS\n[\n19\n]\ncontains over 97k songs from Suno, Udio, and YouTube. While SONICS is a hybrid dataset at the prompt level with real or AI-generated lyrics/music styles, it does not mix human-recorded and AI-generated stems at the audio level.\nFakeMusicCaps\n[\n5\n]\ntargets both detection and attribution using five open-source text-to-music models.\nArtifactBench\n[\n17\n]\nspans 22 generators for codec-aware generalization evaluation, and M6\n[\n14\n]\ncovers multiple generators, domains, languages, genres, and instruments.\nAI-OpenBMAT\n[\n15\n]\naddresses broadcast scenarios with short, speech-masked excerpts, reporting F1-scores below 60% when the SNR of the fake signal is low. This setting is related to ours, as AI-generated stems are mixed with human-recorded sources, but, as opposed to our study, the human-recorded part is considered as noise preventing detection.\nNone of those datasets include\nhybrid\nmusic that mixes AI-generated stems with real stems.\nGenerated Singing Voice Detection in Music Mixtures:\nThe task of detecting whether vocals in a music track are real or AI-generated is the most closely related to ours. SingFake\n[\n25\n]\naddresses this by training several anti-spoofing countermeasure systems, either on source-separated vocals or full mixtures, finding that the benefit of source separation prior to detection is architecture-dependent, with no consistent conclusion across systems. Similarly, cloned singer identification shows that mixture-based models degrade substantially more than vocal-based ones when exposed to cloned voices\n[\n10\n]\n. A key distinction from both works is that our task is more general: rather than targeting just vocal authenticity, we perform\nstem-level\ndetection for potentially any component of the mix.\n3\nHybrid music dataset\nTo our knowledge, no dataset currently exists for hybrid music containing musically coherent stems from real and AI-generated sources.\nWhile building a realistic database in such settings from commercial AI generation services would require a dedicated work (left for future iterations), we decide here to synthesize hybrid mixture through a neural autoencoding of stems taken from a music source separation dataset, following the method in\n[\n1\n,\n2\n]\n.\nWe use MUSDB18-HQ\n[\n18\n]\nsource separation dataset providing multi-stems for 150 tracks, downmixed to isolated vocals\nv\nv\nand accompaniment\na\na\n.\nIn order to synthesize the AI generation of a stem, we apply\nencodec\nautoencoding with 24 kbps at 48 kHz configuration\n[\n9\n]\n.\nThis follows the framework introduced in\n[\n1\n]\n, on which we base our work, and that reported robust detection performance for 3 reference neural codecs, as well as 3 versions of Suno and one of Udio.\nWe thus hypothesize that our experimental work and findings based on a single decoder should provide a robust base framework for extending the experiments to more realistic hybrid mixtures.\nFrom both the original (with subscript\nr\nr\nfor\nreal\n) and the autoencoded (\ng\ng\nfor\ngenerated\n) stems of a given track, we construct four types of coherent mixes:\n•\nHybrid mix with generated vocals\n: encoded vocals with non-encoded accompaniment, noted\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\n.\n•\nHybrid mix with generated accompaniment\n: non-encoded vocals with encoded accompaniment,\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\n.\n•\nFully generated mix\n: both stems encoded,\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\n.\n•\nGenuine mix\n: both stems non-encoded,\nv\nr\n+\na\nr\nv_{r}+a_{r}\n.\nThe mixes are constructed linearly with different mixing gains.\nIn the remainder of this paper, when evaluating stem-specific detectors, we define as\ntarget gain\nin\n{\n−\n12\n,\n−\n6\n,\n0\n,\n+\n6\n,\n+\n12\n}\n\\{-12,-6,0,+6,+12\\}\ndB, the gain applied to the target stem (\ne.g\nthe vocals for an AI vocals detection task) while the other stem’s gain is set to 0 dB (\ne.g\nthe accompaniment).\nFinally, a peak normalization to unit amplitude is applied to the obtained mixture.\nMore complex mixing scenarios, including mastering, are left for future work.\nThe full dataset comprises\n150\n150\nmixtures\n×\n4\n\\times 4\nmix types\n×\n5\n\\times 5\ntarget gains\n=\n3\n,\n000\n=3{,}000\nmixtures.\ntarget gain (dB)\n-12\n-6\n0\n6\n12\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\n12.0\n31.33\n55.33\n78.67\n88.67\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\n97.33\n98.67\n98.67\n99.33\n100.0\nTable 1\n:\nProportion (in %) of tracks detected as fully generated when the reference detector is directly applied to hybrid mixtures, for different mixing gains.\n4\nFully generated AI music detection\nThis section details the training and evaluation of fully generated AI music detectors that will be used in Sections\n5\nand\n6\nfor building stem-specific detectors.\n4.1\nReference detector\nIn this preliminary experiment, we assess the limits of an artifact-based AI detector trained from real and fully generated music, when applied to hybrid music.\nThus, here the task is a global \"generated\nvs.\nreal\" binary classification of a track, with no detection at the stem level.\nTraining on fully generated music:\nAs our reference AI detector, we reproduce the system from\n[\n1\n]\n.\nWe train this detector from scratch using the raw audio files from the Free Music Archive (FMA) medium dataset\n[\n8\n]\nas the negative class (real), and the same files autoencoded with\nencodec\n[\n9\n]\nas the positive class (generated).\nWe use a random train/test split of 70/30.\nBoth real and generated versions of a track are distributed in the same set.\nOn the test set, we obtain a True Positive Rate (TPR) of\n99.79\n%\n99.79\\%\nand a False Positive Rate (FPR) of\n0\n%\n0\\%\n, which is consistent with\n[\n1\n]\n. Note that this task is performed on mixes only: the detector is a single binary classifier that discriminates fully real\nvs.\nfully generated tracks, and does not provide any information about the generation at the stem level.\nInterestingly, when testing on the (unseen) dataset from Section\n3\non\nv\nr\n+\na\nr\nv_{r}+a_{r}\nand\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\nscenarios, the detector achieves perfect accuracy (\n100\n%\n100\\%\nTPR and\n0\n%\n0\\%\nFPR).\nTesting on hybrid mixtures:\nHere we apply the reference AI music detector to hybrid tracks,\ni.e.\nto\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\nand\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\nsubsets from the dataset of Section\n3\n.\nThe results, presented in\nTable\n1\n, show that the artifacts of the generated stem persist in the hybrid mixtures and that their detection is influenced by the target gain and the nature of the stem source. We observe an asymmetry between vocals and accompaniment with more detection in the case\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\n. This is probably because accompaniments that include percussion are usually dominant in high frequency bands where the detection happens (5-16kHz).\nVocals also tend to be sparser over time and might be a minor contributor to the average profile computed by the detection system\n[\n1\n]\n.\nTherefore, while a plain binary detector is unable to distinguish between hybrid and fully generated mixes, this preliminary result suggests that useful detection artifacts are preserved during the mixing operation.\n4.2\nFrom global to local AI detection\nWe here adapt the fully generated AI detector, as presented above, from a global (full track prediction) to a local scale (short segment prediction).\nThis will allow for a finer temporal granularity essential to our proposed approach, as described in the following sections.\nWe thus re-train detectors on short audio chunks of\n1\n1\n,\n2\n2\n,\n5\n5\nand\n10\n10\nseconds from the same FMA-based dataset, keeping the same experimental conditions.\nTable\n2\nreports the AI chunk detection performance for all configurations.\nPerformance remains high with only limited degradations for very short segments where only a fraction of audio is provided.\nChunk dur. (s)\n10\n5\n2\n1\nTPR (%)\n99.733\n99.693\n99.139\n96.860\nFPR (%)\n0.025\n0.081\n0.516\n2.015\nTable 2\n:\nFully AI-generated chunk prediction performance as a function of the audio chunk duration (in seconds).\n5\nDetection of AI-generated stems: A naive approach\nWe now turn to the main task addressed in this work, namely AI-generated stem detection, and introduce a method that provides a prediction score for each stem (vocals or accompaniment) indicating whether or not it was generated.\nThis section explores a naive baseline solution to this task that consists of performing Music Source Separation (MSS) and then applying a plain AI music detector independently to each stem.\n5.1\nBaseline: separate then detect\nHere, we use the dataset presented in Section\n3\n, considering the four mixing scenarios.\nWe first apply\nht-demucs\n[\n20\n]\nto perform 2-stems MSS on the mixtures, and then apply the global reference detector from Section\n4.1\nindependently on each isolated stem.\nThe TPR and FPR for both generated vocals and accompaniment detection tasks are reported as blue bars in\nFigure\n2\nfor the different target gains.\nOverall, the performance is quite poor, especially for the detection of generated vocals.\nOnce again, the performance varies monotonically with respect to the mixing gain of the target stem, with very high FPRs for both vocals and accompaniment detection at lower target gains.\nConfusion matrices, represented in\nFigure\n1\n, highlight that for the hybrid case\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\n, the real vocals are almost always predicted as generated with a FPR of\n94.7\n%\n94.7\\%\n(left matrix).\nThis suggests that artifacts from the generated accompaniment may have spread in the vocal stem through MSS.\nThus, this naive system is unable to discriminate between\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\nand\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\n.\nThe discrimination between\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\nand\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\nis also weak, with a FPR of\n38\n%\n38\\%\nfor the detection of accompaniment in the hybrid case\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\n.\nFigure 1\n:\nConfusion matrices for the naive stem detectors for all mixing cases with target gain\n0\ndB. Correct (resp. False) detection cases are colored in green (resp. red).\n5.2\nQualitative analysis from the local AI detection\nWe hypothesize that the naive approach performs poorly due to the MSS inability to accurately transfer the artifacts to the generated stem only, and propose a qualitative analysis based on the local AI-generated music detector from Section\n4.2\n.\nFigures\n5\n(a) and (b) illustrate the case of a hybrid track with generated vocals (\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\n).\nInterestingly, local detection scores from the mix (blue), vocals (green) and accompaniment (orange) from subfigure (b) are highly correlated, while we would expect here that the accompaniment score remains close to 0 along the whole excerpt.\nThis tends to confirm our assumption that generation artifacts are spread over all separated sources.\nWe must note here that the MSS system can also introduce its own artifacts, potentially of a similar nature to those expected from neural codecs.\nIndeed,\nht-demucs\n[\n20\n]\nis based on an encoder/decoder-like architecture, involving upsampling from a latent space with transposed convolution layers.\nWhile such artifacts are likely to appear, it seems from Figure\n5\n(b) that this did not perturb our experiments: both local detection scores from extracted vocal and accompaniment stems return to 0 after\n177\n~177\ns\ns\n,\ni.e.\nwhen the generated stem is inactive.\nWhile we could consider several, probably limited, improvements to this naive baseline (\ne.g.\ntesting different MSS architectures, retraining a MSS model from hybrid mixtures, or more simply retraining stem-specific detectors from separated sources), in the following section we propose an alternative approach that helps circumventing the exposed weaknesses of this sequential approach.\n6\nProposed approach\nBuilding on preliminary experiments described in Section\n5\n, we propose here a joint/parallel approach that combines the prediction of the local mix detector with the information about the relative time-frequency energy of each stem.\n6.1\nFormalizing the task\nIndependently for each stem, we design a binary classifier that predicts from the audio mixture, at the chunk level (indexed by\nt\nt\n), whether the considered stem was generated or not.\nThe goal is to compute an estimate of\np\n​\n(\nv\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n(resp.\np\n​\n(\na\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n), the posterior probability that the vocal (resp. the accompaniment) stem is generated, given the prediction of the local AI mix detector\nf\nt\nf_{t}\n, and the ratio of energy between the target and complementary stems\nSNR\nt\nb\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\nfor each band\nb\nb\nin the set of all frequency bands considered\nB\nB\n.\nf\nt\nf_{t}\nis obtained directly as the output of the local detector of Section\n4.2\napplied to the input mix chunk\nt\nt\n.\nThe energy quantities used to compute\nSNR\nt\nb\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\nare defined as the sum of the squared magnitudes of the short-time Fourier transform over the considered stems at chunk\nt\nt\nand frequency band\nb\nb\n.\nWe consider two scenarios for estimating\nSNR\nt\nb\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\n:\n•\nMSS SNR\n, where we apply\nht-demucs\non the mix and use the separated stems to compute the source energies.\n•\nOracle SNR\n, where the energy of the sources is directly computed from the available reference stems of the dataset, therefore bypassing the potential degradations and artifacts introduced by MSS.\nIn the following, we train a model at the chunk level\nt\nt\n(thus considering the independence of chunks) for\np\n​\n(\nv\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\nand\np\n​\n(\na\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\n.\nThen, in order to get a prediction at the level of the full track, we compute the average over time of these probabilities and detect the stem as generated if it is above\n0.5\n0.5\n, and as not generated otherwise.\n6.2\nExperiments\nWe use\n2\n2\n-second-long chunks with\n90\n%\n90\\%\noverlap between chunks.\nWe drop chunks with very low energy, considered as silence, as the corresponding\nf\nt\nf_{t}\nand\nSNR\nt\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}\nare unreliable in both train and test sets.\nThis pre-processing removes on average\n∼\n5\n%\n\\sim 5\\%\nof chunks per track, essentially trimming leading and trailing silences on the mix.\nWe train a simple multilayer perceptron model (5 hidden layers, 32 linear units each, ReLu activation, sigmoid on output layer) for AI-generated vocals detection and a second independent one for AI-generated accompaniment detection.\nWe use binary cross-entropy as loss function.\nf\nt\nf_{t}\nand\nSNR\nt\nb\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\ninput features are simply stacked together (total dimension of\nB\n+\n1\nB+1\n) as input to the model.\nWe run 10 cross-validation 70/30 splits, where the split is applied at the track level (\ni.e.\nall mixing variants of a given track are distributed into the same set).\nFor the SNR bands, we use two configurations:\n•\n16 bands with 1 kHz width over the range 0-16 kHz, used for reporting performance in\n6.3\n.\n•\nA single band 5-16 kHz (which corresponds to the band used in\n[\n1\n]\nfor computing the detector’s input features), only used for the qualitative analysis in Section\n6.4\n.\nFigure 2\n:\nTPR (top) and FPR (bottom) metrics for vocals (left) and accompaniment (right) AI-generated stem detections grouped over all mixing configurations, and as a function of the target gain (x-axis).\n6.3\nEvaluation\nTPRs and FPRs are reported in\nFigure\n2\n.\nWe can see that the performance in the Oracle scenario (green bars) is quite high.\nAs expected again, we observe that both metrics degrade as the target gain decreases: detection becomes more difficult when the generated stem has lower energy.\nWe also observe that performance is stem-dependent and that it remains much easier to detect generated accompaniment than generated vocals.\nIn the MSS scenario, general trends are similar (orange bars).\nTPRs are comparable to the Oracle configuration, but we notice a significant FPR degradation for the prediction of generated vocals, especially at low vocals gain.\nIn this realistic configuration, however, performance remains substantially better than the naive method (blue bars).\nFor a more detailed analysis of errors from the the vocals detector, we display in\nFigure\n3\nthe confusion matrices against the\n4\n4\npossible mixing classes at target gain 0dB, for both SNR scenarios.\nWe notice that, once again, the most difficult case is the discrimination between the hybrid mix\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\nand the fully generated mix\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\n.\nThe realistic system using SNR estimated from MSS achieves the most degraded performance when compared to the Oracle SNR configuration.\nHowever, as opposed to the naive method (Fig.\n1\n, left matrix), our system shows a stronger ability to discriminate between the hybrid case\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\nand the fully generated case\nv\ng\n+\na\ng\nv_{g}+a_{g}\n.\nEven in the realistic scenario using MSS-based SNR, for hybrid mixes\nv\nr\n+\na\ng\nv_{r}+a_{g}\nthe FPR drops from\n94.7\n%\n94.7\\%\nfor the naive approach to\n26.2\n%\n26.2\\%\nfor the proposed approach.\nFigure 3\n:\nConfusion matrices for the proposed AI-generated vocals detector for a target gain of\n0\ndB. Influence of MSS (right) over Oracle (left) SNR estimator.\nWe must acknowledge here that Demucs was trained on MUSDB18-HQ.\nThis could possibly result in better local SNR estimates than on data not seen during Demucs training, and in turn lead to overoptimistic performance.\nThis would require retraining Demucs with other data, or collecting another dataset, which is left for future work.\n6.4\nQualitative analysis\nIn\nFigure\n4\n, we display the trained model posteriors\np\n​\n(\nv\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\np(v_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\nand\np\n​\n(\na\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\np(a_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\nfor the simple case where there is a single SNR band from 5 kHz to 16 kHz.\nThe x-axis shows the logit-transformed local mix detection score\nlogit\n​\n(\nf\nt\n)\n=\nlog\n⁡\n(\nf\nt\n/\n(\n1\n−\nf\nt\n)\n)\n\\text{logit}(f_{t})=\\log(f_{t}/(1-f_{t}))\n, mapping the output from\n[\n0\n,\n1\n]\n[0,1]\nto\n]\n−\n∞\n,\n+\n∞\n[\n]-\\infty,+\\infty[\n, where negative (resp. positive) values indicate that the local mix detector predicts the segment as real (resp. generated).\nThe y-axis represents the target stem SNR in dB.\nAlthough the decision frontiers are stem-dependent, a few consistent areas can be interpreted:\nArea 1:\nfor\nSNR\n≫\n0\n\\textrm{SNR}\\gg 0\n, the target stem (noted\ns\n∈\n{\nv\n,\na\n}\ns\\in\\{v,a\\}\n) is the dominant stem, then the prediction of generation for this stem tends towards the prediction of the mix:\np\n​\n(\ns\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\n→\nSNR\nt\nb\n→\n+\n∞\nf\nt\np(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\xrightarrow[\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\\rightarrow+\\infty]{}f_{t}\n.\nFor\nSNR\n≪\n0\n\\textrm{SNR}\\ll 0\n,\ni.e.\nwhen the complementary stem is dominant, we observe three main areas:\nArea 2:\nfor middle prediction scores on the mix (\n0\n≪\nf\nt\n≪\n1\n0\\ll f_{t}\\ll 1\n),\ni.e.\nwhen the mix detector is less confident, the target stem which has lower energy is likely generated.\nOur interpretation is the following: for middle prediction scores in this specific 2-stem mixture scenario, at least one and only one stem has to be generated.\nIndeed, if both were real (resp. generated) the local mix detection score would tend towards 0 (resp. 1).\nThe complementary stem is here dominant and thus less likely to be generated; if it was, it would trigger a greater prediction score towards 1.\nArea 3:\nf\nt\n→\n1\nf_{t}\\rightarrow 1\n, the detector’s score on the mix is very high.\nIt is then likely that either the mix is fully generated, or the complementary dominant stem only is generated.\nThe target stem then has equal chances to be generated or real, hence\np\n​\n(\ns\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\n∼\n0.5\np(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\sim 0.5\n.\nNote that this value is due to the balance of the 4 classes of mix in our dataset, and can be interpreted as an uninformative prior.\nArea 4:\nf\nt\n→\n0\nf_{t}\\rightarrow 0\n, the detector on the mix has a very low prediction value. This means either that both stems are real, or that the energy of the target stem is so low that its generation artifacts disappear in the mix. Then, the target stem\ns\ns\ncan either be generated or real, and\np\n​\n(\ns\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\n∼\n0.5\np(s_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\\sim 0.5\n.\nThis phenomenon appears mostly for vocals, though. For accompaniment,\np\n​\n(\na\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\np(a_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\ndrops to\n0\n, which is likely due to the fact that mixing with a vocals stem, even with high energy, is insufficient to hide generation artifacts (possibly from drums sitting in the hi-frequency range).\nAgain, the value\n0.5\n0.5\ncan still be interpreted as an uninformative prior.\nThis analysis gives an overview of the informative zones for differentiating hybrid mixes. Some of them are intuitive: in an area where a stem is strongly dominant, the prediction on the mix transfers to the stem.\nHowever, some are counterintuitive, as one may consider a low energy ratio for the target stem to result in an uninformative zone, when such a ratio can actually be very informative for middle-valued detection scores.\nAdditional tests suggested that these properties remain valid in the case of\nSNR\nt\nb\n\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b}\nestimated for multiple bands\nb\nb\n.\nThese conclusions also describe hard samples: these are tracks for which the target stem SNR remains low while the prediction score\nf\nt\nf_{t}\nremains extreme.\nOn such samples, the posterior of the low-energy stem is uninformative and does not enable the discrimination between a fully generated mix and a hybrid mix where only one stem with high energy was generated.\nThe larger zone for hard samples in\np\n​\n(\nv\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\nSNR\nt\nb\n)\np(v_{g}|f_{t},\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b})\nmay explain the loss of performance between vocals and accompaniment stem detection.\nFigure 4\n:\nTrained posteriors for the generated vocals (left) and accompaniment (right) detection models.\nX-axis: logit-transformed fake score; how strongly the local detector predicts the mix as fake.\nY-axis: SNR in dB; how loud the target stem is relative to the complementary stem.\nFigure\n5\n(c) depicts the local estimation of\np\n​\n(\nv\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(v_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\nand\np\n​\n(\na\ng\n|\nf\nt\n,\n{\nSNR\nt\nb\n,\nb\n∈\nB\n}\n)\np(a_{g}|f_{t},\\{\\textrm{SNR}_{t}^{b},b\\in B\\})\non a hybrid track\nv\ng\n+\na\nr\nv_{g}+a_{r}\n(generated vocals and real accompaniment).\nThe estimation here is consistently correct with a high generation probability for vocals and a low generation probability for accompaniment.\nThe system becomes less confident for the accompaniment when the vocal activity decreases.\nHowever, after\n177\n​\ns\n177s\n, when only the accompaniment is active, the probability for fake vocals approaches\n0.5\n0.5\n(uninformative chunks, in accordance with leftmost\nFigure\n4\n, Area 4), and the probability for fake accompaniment drops close to\n0\n(low score \n", "numLines": 1225, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1225}}}
